{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nEvery other story in this epic is validated against fixtures and a stateful fake transport. That is\nthe right tier for each of them, but it cannot exercise the failure modes that only appear against a\nreal tracker: actual JQL evaluation over several projects, real per-project permissions and key\nallocation, real issue creation, and — the one that matters most — whether a pass scoped to one\nproject can contaminate another. A multi-project bridge that is wrong in that direction writes real\nissues into the wrong project, which is expensive to detect and worse to undo. This story is the\njustified live canary the shared test-design standard allows for external-service mapping, run once\nthe functionality is otherwise complete.\n\nA first live-Cloud draft of this harness (against the shared REB + DIG Cloud projects) surfaced two\nfatal design flaws that the fake transport had masked, and that make live Cloud the wrong tier:\n\n1. Non-existent project keys parse-error, they do not return empty. The draft modelled the\n   \"configured but empty\" and \"unknown to the tracker\" paths with dummy project keys that exist only\n   in the mapping and never in Jira. Real Jira rejects `project = REBEMPTY` with a JQL parse error\n   (`the value 'rebempty' does not exist for the field 'project'`) and acli exits non-zero, which —\n   because the inbound fetcher fans a per-project query out with no per-project try/except\n   (`fetcher.py` `_build_snapshot`, \"Any exception raised by AcliClient.search_issues() propagates\n   out\") — aborts the ENTIRE pass. The fake transport returned an empty result set for an unknown\n   project, so nothing caught it. There is no product contract that a mapped-but-absent project\n   should be tolerated; the inbound search is deliberately fail-closed, which for a bridge is the\n   safe posture (silently skipping a typo'd project key would hide a real operator error).\n2. Only two real Cloud projects exist (REB, DIG). Faithfully modelling three repo-configuration\n   shapes (one repo / two repos / zero repos), a genuinely empty project, and an unknown-project\n   fail-closed path needs more isolated projects than the two shared production projects can supply,\n   and creating throwaway projects in the shared Cloud instance is not available to this identity.\n\n## Approach\n\nRehearse the whole feature against the ephemeral Jira Data Center instance the existing DC\nharness (`tests/external/live_jira_dc/`) already stands up in CI, where we hold admin rights and can\ncreate and destroy throwaway projects per run. This dissolves both flaws above and — decisively —\nmakes the rehearsal a reproducible, CI-verified green run on every build (the `jira-dc-harness`\njob) instead of a manual live-Cloud step gated on operator attestation. The isolated store copy is\nthe DC harness's existing local/file-based copy (`REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=off`); no S3 remote is involved.\n\nReal throwaway projects, faithfully modelling every path. A per-run fixture provisions four\nscratch projects on the DC instance via the harness's bug-3fe5-hardened provisioning (pinned\n`_PROJECT_TEMPLATE`, then `_assert_project_capabilities` verifies the issue types and fields before\nany test runs — no silent-degrade fallback), each with a random `RBJ`-prefixed key, and tears them\nall down on every exit path:\n\n- P_ONE  -> mapped to one repo (single-repo config),\n- P_TWO  -> mapped to two repos (two-repo config),\n- P_ZERO -> mapped to zero repos (the zero-repo / \"configured but empty repos\" config),\n- P_LEGACY -> the recorded legacy default.\n\nThe \"configured but empty of issues\" path is now a REAL project that simply has no issues in a given\npass (returns `[]` cleanly, as a real empty project does), never a non-existent key. The\n\"unknown to Jira\" path is modelled correctly as its true product behaviour: a project key that was\nnever created is configured in the mapping, and the harness asserts the pass fails closed (the\nper-project search raises and the pass aborts writing nothing) — testing the safe behaviour that\nactually exists rather than a tolerated-empty behaviour that does not.\n\nDry-run gating is the contamination control. Each mutating scenario runs `bridge preview` first\nand asserts the previewed mutation set contains only targets in the intended project, with an\nempty intersection against every other configured project. Only then does `bridge sync` run. A\npreview that names a target outside the intended project fails the scenario before anything is\nwritten — the same fail-closed posture as the cross-project guard, applied at the harness level.\nEvery mutating pass — including any setup sync that binds a ticket — is preview-gated first.\n\nCleanup is designed in, not bolted on, and runs on every exit path. Teardown deletes every\nscratch project (DELETE `/rest/api/2/project/{key}`, poll-until-404) via session-scoped fixture\nfinalizers; deleting a project removes its issues, so no per-issue sweep is needed, and because the\nprojects are per-run random keys no run can strand or delete another run's data. A session guard\nrefuses to start if any prior-run `RBJ*` scratch project leaked. Each finalizer is not considered\nfinished until the project read returns 404.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/_dc_projects.py` (new) — the scratch-project provisioning cluster\n  (`_create_scratch_project`, `_assert_project_capabilities`, `_random_project_key`, delete/poll)\n  extracted from `conftest.py` so it is importable by both `conftest.py` and the new rehearsal,\n  plus a multi-project provisioning helper that yields N verified scratch projects and tears them\n  down. Extraction is behaviour-preserving and also brings `conftest.py` back under the module-size\n  cap.\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/conftest.py` (edit) — import the extracted provisioning helpers so\n  the existing `jira_dc_project` fixture and session leak-guard keep working unchanged.\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_multi_project_rehearsal.py` (new) — the rehearsal harness and its\n  scenarios, plus its per-run fixtures (four scratch projects, the isolated DC store copy mapped for\n  them, the DC-pointed bridge config).\n- `docs/jira-sync-setup.md` (edit) — the operator runbook: the multi-project rehearsal now runs\n  against the ephemeral DC instance in CI (and how to run it locally with `make jira-dc-up`),\n  replacing the live-Cloud runbook section.\n- `tests/external/live_multi_project/conftest.py` (delete) and\n  `tests/external/live_multi_project/test_multi_project_rehearsal.py` (delete) — the flawed\n  live-Cloud-only draft, replaced by the DC rehearsal above.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: a live end-to-end rehearsal against a real Jira Data Center instance, which the shared standard\npermits for external-service mapping when a fake cannot reach the mechanism — here, genuine\ncross-project isolation and real JQL evaluation over several projects. It is gated to the opt-in\nexternal suite (`REBAR_RUN_EXTERNAL=1`, the DC-readiness sentinel) and runs in the dedicated\n`jira-dc-harness` CI job (`pytest -m external tests/external/live_jira_dc`), which brings the DC\ninstance up on a native-amd64 runner. A new file under that directory is collected automatically.\n\nScenarios, each preview-gated then applied:\n\n- Inbound from two projects. An issue created in P_ONE and one in P_TWO are both ingested in a\n  single pass; each resulting ticket records the project it came from and the repos mapped to that\n  project.\n- Outbound to two projects. Two rebar tickets, one per project, each land as an issue in their\n  own project and nowhere else.\n- Not-synced. A ticket with an explicit empty project produces no issue in any project, and no\n  outbound mutation naming it appears in the preview.\n- Legacy resolution. A ticket with the project field absent syncs to the recorded legacy default\n  (P_LEGACY) and to no other project.\n- Repo-config variety, both directions. Inbound tickets from the single-repo (P_ONE), two-repo\n  (P_TWO) and zero-repo (P_ZERO) projects receive exactly those repo lists; outbound tickets created\n  under each config reach the correct project regardless of their repos value, confirming repos never\n  influences routing.\n- Contamination guard, the negative control. An outbound update whose target resolves to a\n  project outside the configured scope (a stale binding / foreign id label) is caught at two layers.\n  First, `bridge preview` surfaces the offending target in its planned mutation set — dry-run does\n  not reach the write-path guard, so preview does not itself raise; the harness's empty-intersection\n  assertion flags the foreign target and the scenario stops before any write. Second, driving the\n  real product guard, `bridge sync` on that same staged mutation raises `CrossProjectTargetError` from\n  the write path (`_apply_batch`) and writes nothing — verified by querying the projects afterward and\n  confirming no issue changed.\n- Unknown project fails closed. With a project key that was never created in the DC instance added\n  to the mapping, a bridge pass fails closed — the per-project search errors and the pass aborts —\n  writing nothing to any project. This pins the deliberate fail-closed posture (a mapped-but-absent\n  project is a loud, immediate error, not a silently tolerated empty).\n- Promote-only. Setting a project on an unbound ticket then syncing creates the issue; attempting\n  to change a bound ticket's project is refused and the existing binding is unchanged.\n- Capability stamp. With multiple projects in the mapping, `.store-compat.json` carries\n  `multi-project-bridge`, and a binary lacking that capability refuses to write to the rehearsal\n  store.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The rehearsal provisions its throwaway scratch projects with the hardened pinned template and\n      verifies their capabilities before any scenario runs; checked by asserting each created\n      project reports the required issue types/fields (a degrade fails provisioning, not a later\n      scenario).\n- [ ] Issues created in two scratch projects are both ingested in one pass with the correct source\n      project and repos recorded; checked by querying both projects and asserting both tickets'\n      fields.\n- [ ] Rebar-created tickets targeting each project land in that project only; checked by querying the\n      projects and asserting each issue exists in exactly one.\n- [ ] A ticket with an explicit empty project creates no issue in any project; checked by a post-run\n      query of every scratch project finding no issue for it.\n- [ ] All three repo configurations (one / two / zero repos) produce the expected repos on inbound\n      tickets and correct routing on outbound ones; checked per configuration.\n- [ ] An outbound update targeting a project outside the configured scope is surfaced in `bridge\n      preview`'s planned mutation set (flagged by the empty-intersection assertion) and, on `bridge\n      sync`, raises `CrossProjectTargetError` from the write-path guard and changes nothing live;\n      checked by comparing the projects' issue state before and after the refused sync.\n- [ ] A project key never created in the DC instance, when configured in the mapping, makes a bridge\n      pass fail closed and write nothing; checked by asserting the pass errors and a post-run query of\n      the real projects shows no change.\n- [ ] Every scenario's preview contained only targets in its intended project; checked by asserting\n      an empty intersection against every other configured project for each preview.\n- [ ] The whole rehearsal runs green in the `jira-dc-harness` CI job against the ephemeral DC\n      instance; checked by the job passing (the run is not skipped — the fail-on-all-skip canary\n      enforces that at least one live scenario executed).\n- [ ] Every scratch project the run created is deleted on teardown and a read of each returns 404,\n      including when a scenario fails mid-run; checked by injecting a post-creation failure into one\n      scenario and asserting all scratch projects are gone (404) after teardown."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786743554883069001,"uuid":"5e9d8e2c-42b6-453e-9165-cf2a10800a82"}